But wait - let me re-examine. The check `it + 4 >= end` returns false (error). So we proceed when `it + 4 < end`. That means `it + 4 <= end - 1`, so `it + 3 <= end - 2`. Reading `it[3]` is safe.

Hmm, but what about the surrogate pair case? After `it += 4`, the check is `if (it + 6 >= end)`. So we proceed when `it + 6 < end`, i.e., `it + 5 <= end - 1`. Then `std::memcpy(&u, it, 2)` reads `it[0]` and `it[1]`, safe. Then `it += 2`, and `hex_to_u32(it)` reads `it[0]` through `it[3]`. Since `it + 5 <= end - 1`, we have `it + 3 <= end - 3`, safe.

OK so the unicode handling seems fine. Let me look at the actual string parsing in the non-padded version more carefully. The issue is "READ 5" which suggests reading 5 bytes past the buffer. Let me look at the `continue_decode` section in the non-padded version (line 848+ in read.hpp):

Looking at line 933: `value.resize(n + string_padding_bytes)` where `n = size_t(it - start)`. Then the decode loop does `std::memcpy(p, start, 8)`. This reads 8 bytes from `start`. But `start` can be up to `it - 1`. Since `available_padding = end - it >= 8`, we have `end >= it + 8`, so `start + 8 <= it + 7 <= end - 1`. Safe.

